WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.847
Propose the first and last ring years.

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:07.947
Identify at least three matching features and compare another placement.

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:13.618
Maintain consistent annual spacing when aligning the series, and check whether a promising

00:00:13.632 --> 00:00:16.920
match creates contradictions elsewhere.

00:00:17.723 --> 00:00:22.336
A: 1962–1991, 30 rings.

00:00:22.791 --> 00:00:25.811
Several features align at matching intervals.

00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:30.567
If your answer differs, inspect the sequence and endpoint counting.

00:00:31.028 --> 00:00:35.188
Compare the reasoning, not only the selected start year.

00:00:35.991 --> 00:00:39.505
The first part may match while the later part diverges.

00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:44.710
Investigate missed boundaries, locally absent rings or false rings.

00:00:45.164 --> 00:00:49.210
Do not insert or delete values merely to improve a match.

00:00:49.664 --> 00:00:55.467
If alignment works initially but shifts later, investigate boundary errors, measurements and

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.136
locally absent increments.

00:00:58.939 --> 00:01:02.262
Compare segments of the 31 observed rings.

00:01:02.718 --> 00:01:06.810
Can one constant shift align both early and late segments?.

00:01:07.112 --> 00:01:11.178
Record where to re-examine boundaries and possible causes.

00:01:11.639 --> 00:01:13.737
Pause to locate the shift.

00:01:14.191 --> 00:01:20.748
Before adding an increment, consider which specimen observations would verify that decision.

00:01:21.551 --> 00:01:29.052
B spans 1958–1989: 31 observed rings across 32 calendar years.

00:01:29.514 --> 00:01:35.626
Reserve the 1974 position between 1973 and 1975.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.994
Keep it missing; do not invent an observed width of zero.

00:01:40.448 --> 00:01:44.981
The missing increment is deliberately specified in this synthetic example.

00:01:45.435 --> 00:01:50.704
Real decisions need support from other radii or independent trees.

00:01:51.507 --> 00:01:53.922
Another radius of the same tree.

00:01:54.378 --> 00:01:58.443
Investigate local absences and boundary-reading errors.

00:01:58.904 --> 00:02:00.371
A different tree.

00:02:00.825 --> 00:02:03.437
Check whether the pattern is reproduced.

00:02:03.891 --> 00:02:06.305
A reference excluding the unknown.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:12.240
Do not use agreement with a reference already containing that sample as independent proof.

00:02:12.693 --> 00:02:17.272
Check independent material as well as agreement with the same reference chronology to

00:02:17.286 --> 00:02:19.868
strengthen the dating evidence.
